# 0004 — Dolby Vision RPU passthrough for the hevc_vaapi encoder.
#
# LINUX ONLY, and the reason is not the patch — it is the pipeline. VAAPI is linux-only here:
# builder/scripts.d/50-vaapi/50-libva.sh:7 returns -1 for any non-linux target and only echoes
# --enable-vaapi when $TARGET == linux*, and msys2/PKGBUILD/ contains no libva package at all.
# So hevc_vaapi does not exist in either windows build.
#
# These therefore print `skip` on the two windows jobs. Do NOT "fix" that into a failure: release
# depends on all four build jobs, so an unconditional hevc_vaapi check would block every publish,
# and it costs a 2.5 hour cycle to discover.
#
# Conversely, on linux this is a HARD failure, not a skip. verify-binary.sh asks whether the
# binary can describe the encoder at all before it looks at any option, and a missing hevc_vaapi
# fails the group. It is deliberately not gated on "is hevc_vaapi in -encoders?" — that would
# degrade a linux build that lost VAAPI into a silent skip, which is the exact failure this gate
# exists to catch.
#
# What these three assertions prove is registration: the options exist in the encoder's static
# AVOption table, so the patch applied and compiled in. They cannot prove the RPU is correct.
#
# (This header used to say "the patch is unverified on hardware". That stopped being true: it was
# verified on an RX 9070 XT, and the record is in docs/patches/0004-dolby-vision-hevc-vaapi.md.)

encoder-option  linux    hevc_vaapi  dolbyvision
encoder-option  linux    hevc_vaapi  dv_l5
encoder-option  linux    hevc_vaapi  dv_l5_canvas
